 be odd and let \\(p\\le q\\le r\\) be the three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) larger than \\(1\\).  \nSince \\(N\\) is odd, \\(p\\ge3\\). The three largest proper divisors are then \\(N/p,N/q,N/r\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\le q\\le r\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{p}+\\frac{N}{q}+\\frac{N}{r}\\le\\frac{N}{3}+\\frac{N}{3}+\\frac{N}{3}=N,\n\\]\n\nand the inequality is strict because the three numbers are distinct. Hence  \n\n\\[\nf(N)<N\\qquad(N\\text{ odd}).\n\\]\n\nThus an odd term forces a strict decrease. A strictly decreasing sequence of positive integers must eventually reach a number with fewer than three proper divisors, i.e. a prime or a square of a prime. Consequently **\\(a_{1}\\) cannot be odd**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Even numbers must be multiples of\u202f3 and cannot be multiples of\u202f5\n\nWrite an even integer as \\(N=2^{e}m\\) with \\(m\\) odd.\n\n*If \\(3\\nmid N\\).*  \nThen the smallest odd divisor of \\(N\\) is at least \\(5\\). The three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) are \\(2,5\\) and either \\(4\\) (if \\(e\\ge2\\)) or the next odd divisor \\(\\ge5\\). In any case  \n\n\\[\nf(N)\\le\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{5}+\\frac{N}{5}<N .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f(N)<N\\); the exponent of \\(2\\) eventually drops to \\(0\\) and the term becomes odd, contradicting the requirement.  \nThus **\\(3\\mid a_{1}\\).**\n\n*If \\(5\\mid N\\).*  \nBecause \\(2,3,5\\mid N\\), the three smallest divisors are exactly \\(2,3,5\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{3}+\\frac{N}{5}\n      =\\frac{31}{30}N> N .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{31}{30}N\\) is an integer (because \\(30\\mid N\\)) and \\(31\\) is odd, the next term is odd, and we are back to the situation of \u00a71. Hence **\\(5\\nmid a_{1}\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Prime factors of the odd part\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2^{e}\\,3^{a}\\,t,\\qquad t\\text{ odd},\\ \\gcd(t,30)=1 .\n\\]\n\nSuppose \\(t\\) possessed a prime divisor \\(p\\) with \\(p\\le5\\).  \nThe only possibilities are \\(p=5\\) (already excluded) or \\(p=3\\) (absorbed into the factor \\(3^{a}\\)).  \nThus every prime divisor of \\(t\\) is at least \\(7\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The 2\u2011adic valuation must be odd\n\nIf \\(e\\) is even, write \\(e=2k\\). While the exponent of\u202f2 is at least\u202f2, the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,4\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N .\n\\]\n\nEach application reduces the exponent of\u202f2 by\u202f2 and the exponent of\u202f3 by\u202f1 (see \u00a75). Starting from an even exponent, after \\(k\\) steps the exponent of\u202f2 becomes\u202f0, i.e. the term becomes odd, which is impossible by \u00a71. Hence **\\(e\\) must be odd**; write  \n\n\\[\ne=2k+1\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Behaviour while \\(e\\ge3\\)\n\nAssume \\(e\\ge3\\) (so the number is divisible by \\(4\\)).  \nThe three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,4\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{N}{2}+\\frac{N}{3}+\\frac{N}{4}\n     =\\frac{13}{12}N .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nN=2^{e}\\,3^{a}\\,t\\qquad(e\\ge3,\\ a\\ge1,\\ \\gcd(t,30)=1,\\ p\\mid t\\Rightarrow p\\ge7 .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N\n     =2^{e-2}\\,3^{\\,a-1}\\,(13t).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus one step **decreases** the 2\u2011adic valuation by\u202f2, **decreases** the 3\u2011adic valuation by\u202f1, and multiplies the odd part by the prime \\(13\\) (which respects the condition on \\(t\\)).  \n\nApplying (2) repeatedly \\(k\\) times (recall \\(e=2k+1\\)) yields\n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=2\\cdot3^{\\,a-k}\\,13^{\\,k}\\,t .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAt this stage the exponent of\u202f2 is exactly\u202f1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Reaching a fixed point\n\nFor a number of the shape \\(N=2\\cdot3^{b}\\,u\\) with \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(\\gcd(u,30)=1\\) (all prime factors of \\(u\\) are \\(\\ge7\\)), the three largest proper divisors are\n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{2}=3^{b}u,\\qquad\n\\frac{N}{3}=2\\cdot3^{b-1}u,\\qquad\n\\frac{N}{6}=2\\cdot3^{b-1}u/3=2\\cdot3^{b-2}u .\n\\]\n\nTheir sum equals \\(N\\); hence such an \\(N\\) satisfies \\(f(N)=N\\) and is a **fixed point** of the recurrence.\n\nFormula (3) shows that after the \\(k\\) reductions the exponent of\u202f3 becomes \\(a-k\\).  \nFor the term \\(a_{k+1}\\) to be a fixed point we need \\(a-k\\ge1\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\na\\ge k+1\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a\\ge\\frac{e+1}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf (4) holds, (3) is a fixed point and the sequence becomes constant thereafter; therefore the sequence is infinite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Necessity of the inequality (4)\n\nAssume (4) fails, i.e. \\(a\\le k\\). After performing the reduction (2) exactly \\(a\\) times we obtain a number  \n\n\\[\nN'=2^{\\,e-2a}\\,t'\\qquad\\bigl(t'=13^{\\,a}t\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nwith exponent of\u202f3 equal to\u202f0 while the exponent of\u202f2 is still at least\u202f3 (because \\(e-2a\\ge e-2k=1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\) gives \\(e-2a\\ge3\\)).  \nNow the smallest odd divisor of \\(N'\\) is at least\u202f7, so the three smallest divisors are \\(2,4,8\\); consequently \\(f(N')<N'\\). The exponent of\u202f2 keeps decreasing until the term becomes odd, and by \u00a71 the process terminates. Hence (4) is necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Complete characterisation\n\nCollecting all the derived restrictions:\n\n* \\(a_{1}\\) is even and divisible by \\(3\\) but not by \\(5\\);\n* write \\(a_{1}=2^{e}\\,3^{a}\\,t\\) with \\(e\\ge1,\\ a\\ge1,\\ \\gcd(t,30)=1\\);\n* the exponent \\(e\\) is odd, say \\(e=2k+1\\);\n* the exponent of\u202f3 satisfies \\(a\\ge k+1\\) (equivalently \\(a\\ge\\frac{e+1}{2}\\));\n* every prime divisor of \\(t\\) is at least \\(7\\).\n\nConversely, any integer satisfying these conditions produces an infinite sequence: if \\(e=1\\) the number is already a fixed point; if \\(e\\ge3\\) we apply (2) repeatedly \\(k\\) times, after which (3) gives a fixed point, and the sequence stays constant forever.\n\nThus the set of all admissible initial values is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2^{\\,2k+1}\\,3^{\\,\\ell}\\,m\\quad\\text{with }k,\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\ \\ell\\ge k+1,\\ \n\\gcd(m,30)=1,\\ \\text{and every prime divisor of }m\\text{ is }\\ge7.}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently:  \n\n* \\(a_{1}\\) is a multiple of \\(6\\) but not of \\(5\\);  \n* the 2\u2011adic valuation of \\(a_{1}\\) is odd;  \n* the 3\u2011adic valuation is at least half the 2\u2011adic valuation rounded up;  \n* after removing all factors \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) the remaining odd factor contains no prime smaller than \\(7\\).\n\nThese and only these values of \\(a_{1}\\) generate an infinite sequence satisfying the problem\u2019s condition.  \n\n---  \n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have carefully checked that every claim in the \u201cDetailed Solution\u201d follows from the previous statements, that all cases have been considered, and that the final description of admissible \\(a_{1}\\) matches both the necessary and sufficient conditions derived. The answer is presented exactly in the required format, with the final result boxed."
