nite is that the\n   three smallest divisors are exactly \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n4. Characterise the integers whose three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,6\\).\n   They are precisely the numbers\n   \\[\n   N=6k\\qquad\\text{with }k\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Indeed, \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) must divide \\(N\\); the next divisor after \\(3\\) is\n   \\(6\\) provided that \\(4\\) (i.e. an extra factor \\(2\\)) and \\(5\\) do not\n   divide \\(N\\).  Condition (2) is therefore necessary and sufficient.\n\nThus the sequence can be infinite **iff** \\(a_{1}\\) satisfies (2); in that\ncase the sequence is constant.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}=6k\\text{ where }k\\text{ is odd and }5\\nmid k\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{n}=N\\;(>1),\\qquad d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots <d_{t}=N\n\\]\n\nbe the complete list of positive divisors of \\(N\\) (so \\(d_{1}=1\\)).\nBecause \\(N\\) has at least three proper divisors, \\(t\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A convenient description of the rule  \n\nThe three **largest** proper divisors of \\(N\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{p_{1}},\\;\\frac{N}{p_{2}},\\;\\frac{N}{p_{3}},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\np_{1}<p_{2}<p_{3}\n\\]\n\nare the three **smallest** divisors of \\(N\\) that are larger than \\(1\\)\n(they are the “partners’’ of the three largest proper divisors in the\nusual divisor‑pairing \\(d\\leftrightarrow N/d\\)).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{p_{1}}+\\frac1{p_{2}}+\\frac1{p_{3}}\\Bigr)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand the three numbers \\(p_{1},p_{2},p_{3}\\) depend only on \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  When does the sequence stay constant?  \n\nEquation (1) shows that the sequence is constant exactly when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{p_{1}}+\\frac1{p_{2}}+\\frac1{p_{3}}=1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll solutions of (2) in positive integers are well known; a short\ninspection gives  \n\n\\[\n(p_{1},p_{2},p_{3})=(2,3,6)\n\\]\n\nas the only possibility (the other Egyptian‑fraction solutions such as\n\\((2,4,4)\\) are impossible because the three smallest divisors of a\npositive integer are distinct).  \n\nHence a number \\(N\\) yields a constant sequence iff its three smallest\ndivisors larger than \\(1\\) are \\(2,3,6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Description of the numbers whose three smallest divisors are\n\\(2,3,6\\)\n\nLet \\(N\\) be such a number.  \nBecause \\(2\\mid N\\) and \\(3\\mid N\\) we can write \\(N=6k\\).  \nIf \\(4\\mid N\\) then \\(4\\) would be a divisor between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\);\ntherefore \\(4\\nmid N\\), i.e. \\(k\\) is odd.  \nIf \\(5\\mid N\\) then \\(5\\) would also lie between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\); hence\n\\(5\\nmid N\\).  \n\nConversely, if \\(N=6k\\) with \\(k\\) odd and \\(5\\nmid k\\), then the only\ndivisors of \\(N\\) that are \\(\\le6\\) are \\(1,2,3,6\\); thus the three\nsmallest divisors larger than \\(1\\) are indeed \\(2,3,6\\).  \n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N=6k\\text{ with }k\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid k}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nis exactly the set of numbers for which the sequence is constant.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  All other numbers lead to a contradiction  \n\nAssume now that \\(a_{1}=N\\) does **not** satisfy (3).  \nLet \\((p_{1},p_{2},p_{3})\\) be the three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) larger\nthan \\(1\\).  \nFrom (1) we consider the value  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\frac1{p_{1}}+\\frac1{p_{2}}+\\frac1{p_{3}} .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.  \\(S>1\\).**  \n\nThen \\(a_{2}=NS>N\\); the sequence is strictly increasing.\nIf \\(p_{1}\\ge4\\) we would have  \n\n\\[\nS\\le\\frac14+\\frac15+\\frac16<1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting \\(S>1\\); hence \\(p_{1}=2\\).  \nIf \\(p_{2}\\ge5\\) then  \n\n\\[\nS\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac16<1,\n\\]\n\nagain impossible, so \\(p_{2