ion.**  \n   The only admissible initial values are precisely the numbers described in\n   (3). For each such value the sequence is constant, satisfying the required\n   condition for all \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{1},a_{2},\\dots\n\\]\n\nbe an infinite sequence of positive integers such that  \n\n* each term has at least three proper divisors, and  \n* for every \\(n\\ge 1\\)\n\n\\[\na_{n+1}= \\text{(sum of the three largest proper divisors of }a_{n}\\text{)} .\n\\tag{S}\n\\]\n\nFor a composite integer \\(N\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n1<d_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}<\\dots<d_{t}\n\\]\n\nthe proper divisors of \\(N\\) larger than \\(1\\).  \nThe three largest proper divisors are \\(N/d_{1},\\,N/d_{2},\\,N/d_{3}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\na_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Fixed points\n\nEquation (1) yields a fixed point precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}=1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe only triple of positive integers satisfying (2) is \\((2,3,6)\\):\nindeed \\(1/2+1/3+1/6=1\\) and any other triple gives a sum different from\n\\(1\\) (a short elementary check of all possibilities with \\(d_{1}\\ge 2\\) shows\nthis).  \n\nConsequently a number \\(N\\) is a fixed point of the transformation (S) iff its\nthree smallest proper divisors are exactly \\(2,3,6\\).\n\nThe condition “\\(2,3,6\\) are the three smallest proper divisors” is equivalent\nto  \n\n* \\(2\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N\\) (so \\(6\\mid N\\));\n* \\(4\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(4\\) would be smaller than \\(6\\));\n* \\(5\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(5\\) would be smaller than \\(6\\)).\n\nWriting \\(N=6k\\) we obtain that \\(k\\) must be odd (otherwise \\(4\\mid N\\)) and\n\\(5\\nmid k\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N=6k\\text{ with }k\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid k}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(N\\) of the form (3) we have \\(a_{n+1}=N\\); the sequence is\nconstant and each term possesses at least three proper divisors (namely\n\\(1,2,3,6,\\dots\\)). Thus all numbers described by (3) are admissible initial\nvalues.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  All other numbers eventually fail\n\nLet \\(N\\) be a positive integer **not** of the form (3).  \nWe prove that starting from \\(N\\) the process (S) cannot be continued\nindefinitely.\n\n---\n\n##### Case I – \\(N\\) odd  \n\nThen \\(d_{1}\\ge 3\\). The three smallest proper divisors are odd, so\n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n\\le \\frac13+\\frac15+\\frac17=\\frac{71}{105}<1 .\n\\]\n\nBy (1) we obtain \\(a_{n+1}<N\\). The new term is also odd, and the inequality is\nstrict, so the sequence is strictly decreasing while staying in the set of odd\ncomposites. Since there are only finitely many odd composites below any given\ninteger, after finitely many steps we must reach a number that does **not** have\nthree proper divisors – i.e. a prime or a square of a prime. In either case the\ndefinition of the sequence breaks down. Hence an odd initial value cannot give\nan infinite sequence.\n\n---\n\n##### Case II – \\(N\\) even but \\(3\\nmid N\\)\n\nWrite \\(N=2^{\\alpha}m\\) with \\(m\\) odd and \\(\\alpha\\ge 1\\).\n\n*If \\(\\alpha\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(4\\mid N\\); the three smallest proper divisors are\n\\(2,4\\) and some divisor \\(d\\ge5\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{4}+\\frac1{5}=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n\\]\n\nso again \\(a_{n+1}<N\\).\n\n*If \\(\\alpha=1\\)*, then the smallest proper divisors are \\(2\\) and the two\nsmallest odd divisors of \\(m\\), each at least \\(5\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{5}+\\frac1{7}<1,\n\\]\n\nand we again have a strict decrease.  \n\nThus in both subcases the sequence strictly decreases until it becomes odd,\nafter which Case I applies and forces termination. Therefore no even number\nthat is not a multiple of \\(3\\) can start an infinite sequence.\n\n---\n\n##### Case III – \\(N\\) divisible by \\(6\\) but not of the form (3)\n\nHere \\(d_{1}=2,\\ d_{2}=3\\). Because \\(N\\) is not of the form (3), either\n\\(4\\mid N\\) or \\(5\\mid N\\).\n\n*If \\(4\\mid N\\)*, then the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,4\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{4}=\\frac{13}{12}>1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a_{n+1}>N\\). The new term is even but **not** divisible by \\(3\\)\n(because \\(N/4\\) is not a multiple of \\(3\\)). We are now in Case II, which\nforces eventual failure.\n\n*If \\(5\\mid N\\) (and \\(4\\nmid N\\))* the three smallest proper divisors are\n\\(2,3,5\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{2}+\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{5}=\\frac{31}{30}>1 .\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(a_{n+1}>N\\); the resulting integer is not a multiple of \\(6\\), so we\nfall back to Case I or II, both of which lead to termination.\n\nThus any integer divisible by \\(6\\) that does not satisfy (3) also leads,\nafter finitely many steps, to a term lacking three proper divisors.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Synthesis\n\nThe exhaustive analysis shows:\n\n* If the initial term \\(a_{1}\\) is of the form \\(6k\\) with \\(k\\) odd and\n  \\(5\\nmid k\\), the sequence is constant and satisfies the required condition\n  for all \\(n\\).\n\n* For every other positive integer the process (S) inevitably produces, after a\n  finite number of steps, a term that has fewer than three proper divisors,\n  making the definition impossible to continue.\n\nHence the set of all admissible initial values is exactly the set described in\n(3).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}=6k\\text{ where }k\\text{ is odd and }5\\nmid k\\;}\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the numbers whose three smallest proper divisors are\n\\(2,3,6\\); for each such number the sequence remains constant."
