----------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  The case \\((d_{1},d_{2},d_{3})=(2,3,4)\\)\n\nHere \\(N\\) is even, divisible by \\(4\\) and **not** divisible by \\(3\\).\nFrom (3),\n\n\\[\nf(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14\\Bigr)=\\frac{13}{12}N>N .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(N=2^{\\alpha}m\\) with \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\) and \\(m\\) odd, \\(3\\nmid m\\).\nEquation (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N=2^{\\alpha-2}\\cdot13\\cdot m .\n\\]\n\nThe integer \\(f(N)\\) is **not** divisible by \\(3\\) (the factor \\(13\\) is not),\nso its three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,4\\) and either \\(8\\) (if\n\\(\\alpha\\ge3\\)) or an odd divisor \\(\\ge5\\).  In any event the third smallest\ndivisor of \\(f(N)\\) is at least \\(7\\); consequently the next term\n\\(a_{n+2}=f(f(N))\\) is strictly smaller than \\(f(N)\\) by the argument of\n\u00a73.1.  Hence the sequence cannot stay infinite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  The case \\((d_{1},d_{2},d_{3})=(2,3,5)\\)\n\nNow \\(N\\) is divisible by \\(2,3,5\\) but not by \\(4\\).\nFrom (3),\n\n\\[\nf(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15\\Bigr)=\\frac{31}{30}N>N .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(N=30k\\) with \\(k\\) odd and \\(5\\nmid k\\).  Then (9) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=30k+\\;k=31k .\n\\]\n\nThe number \\(31k\\) is **not** divisible by \\(3\\) (because \\(31\\equiv1\\pmod3\\))\nand is not divisible by \\(5\\).  Its smallest proper divisor is \\(2\\); the\nsecond one is \\(31\\) (since \\(k\\) is odd and \\(\\ge1\\)).  Thus the third\nsmallest proper divisor is at least \\(7\\).  Again we fall under \u00a73.1 and the\nsequence must start decreasing, which eventually forces a term with fewer\nthan three proper divisors \u2013 impossible for an infinite sequence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe analysis of \u00a7\u00a73.1\u20133.3 shows that an infinite sequence satisfying the\nconditions can contain **no** term whose three smallest proper divisors are\ndifferent from \\((2,3,6)\\).  Consequently every term must be a fixed point of\nthe map \\(f\\).  By (7) the fixed points are exactly the numbers\n\n\\[\n6k\\qquad\\text{with }k\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid k .\n\\]\n\nIf the initial term \\(a_{1}\\) belongs to this set, then \\(a_{2}=a_{1}\\) and\nthe whole sequence is constant; each term has the proper divisors\n\\(2,3,6\\) (and possibly more), so the hypothesis is fulfilled for all\n\\(n\\).\n\nConversely, if \\(a_{1}\\) is **not** of the above form, the first term has\nthree smallest proper divisors different from \\((2,3,6)\\); by the discussion\nin \u00a73 the sequence inevitably reaches a term with fewer than three proper\ndivisors, contradicting the requirement that the sequence be infinite.\nHence no other initial value is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the complete set of admissible initial values is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6k\\ \\text{where }k\\text{ is odd and }5\\nmid k } .\n\\]"
