hen  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N=13v. \\tag{L4}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, each application of \\(f\\) reduces the exponent of the prime\u202f2 in the current term by exactly\u202f1 while leaving the exponent of the prime\u202f3 unchanged.\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2,\n\n\\[\nf(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14\\Bigr)=\\frac{13}{12}N.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(12=2^{2}\\cdot3\\), writing \\(N=2^{2}3\\,v\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=13\\cdot2^{0}3^{0}v=13v,\n\\]\n\nso the factor \\(2^{2}\\) disappears and the factor\u202f3 disappears as well; the only powers of\u202f2 left come from those already present in \\(v\\).  Hence the exponent of\u202f2 is reduced by\u202f2 (the two coming from the division by\u202f12) and then increased by\u202f0 (13 is odd), i.e. reduced by\u202f2.  Because the original exponent of\u202f2 in \\(N\\) is at least\u202f2, after one application the exponent becomes at least\u202f0; after each further application the exponent is reduced by\u202f1 (the division by\u202f12 contributes two 2\u2019s, the multiplication by\u202f13 contributes none).  Consequently after exactly \\(m\\) successive applications the exponent of\u202f2 becomes\u202f1, at which point the three smallest proper divisors change to \\((2,3,6)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma\u202f5 (Fixed points).  \nA positive integer \\(M\\) satisfies \\(f(M)=M\\) iff its three smallest proper divisors are \\((2,3,6)\\).  This holds precisely for  \n\n\\[\nM=6\\,t,\\qquad t\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid t. \\tag{L5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u202f2 the equality \\(f(M)=M\\) forces \\((d_{1},d_{2},d_{3})=(2,3,6)\\).  The conditions \u201c\\(2\\mid M\\) and \\(3\\mid M\\)\u201d give the factor\u202f6.  The absence of a divisor\u202f4 forces the exponent of\u202f2 to be exactly\u202f1, i.e. the remaining factor \\(t\\) is odd.  The absence of a divisor\u202f5 forces \\(5\\nmid t\\).  Conversely, any number of the form \\(6t\\) with \\(t\\) odd and \\(5\\nmid t\\) has precisely the three smallest proper divisors \\((2,3,6)\\), whence \\(f(M)=M\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma\u202f6 (All admissible initial values).  \nA positive integer \\(a_{1}\\) generates an infinite sequence satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions **iff**  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,m}\\,t\\qquad(m\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;t\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid t). \\tag{L6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Forward direction.*  \nAssume the sequence \\((a_{n})\\) is infinite and each term has at least three proper divisors.  \nLet \\(a_{k}\\) be the first term whose three smallest proper divisors are not \\((2,3,6)\\).  By Lemma\u202f3 the triple \\((2,3,5)\\) cannot occur, so the only possibility is \\((2,3,4)\\).  Lemma\u202f4 shows that each application of \\(f\\) reduces the exponent of\u202f2 by\u202f1 while preserving the exponent of\u202f3.  Consequently after a finite number \\(m\\) of such steps the exponent of\u202f2 becomes\u202f1, at which point the triple becomes \\((2,3,6)\\) and the sequence stabilises at a fixed point.  Tracing the computation backwards yields  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,m}\\,t,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t\\) is the odd part of the eventual fixed point and, by Lemma\u202f5, \\(5\\nmid t\\).\n\n*Reverse direction.*  \nLet \\(a_{1}\\) be of the form (L6).  Write \\(a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,m}\\,t\\) with the stated conditions.  \nIf \\(m=0\\) then \\(a_{1}=6t\\) satisfies Lemma\u202f5, so the sequence is constant.  \nIf \\(m\\ge1\\) then \\(a_{1}\\) is divisible by \\(12\\); its three smallest proper divisors are \\((2,3,4)\\).  Applying Lemma\u202f4 repeatedly \\(m\\) times yields  \n\n\\[\na_{m+1}=6t,\n\\]\n\nwhich is a fixed point by Lemma\u202f5.  Every intermediate term is divisible by both\u202f2 and\u202f3, and either by\u202f4 (when the exponent of\u202f2 is at least\u202f2) or by\u202f6 (when the exponent of\u202f2 equals\u202f1); hence each term possesses at least the proper divisors \\(2,3\\) and one of \\(4\\) or \\(6\\), i.e. at least three proper divisors.  After the \\(m\\)-th step the sequence becomes constant, so it is infinite and satisfies the required condition.\n\nThus the numbers described in (L6) are precisely the admissible initial values. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Conclusion  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f6 the set of all possible values of \\(a_{1}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,m}\\,t\\qquad\\bigl(m\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;t\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid t\\bigr)} .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence becomes constant after exactly \\(m\\) steps (or immediately when \\(m=0\\)), and every term indeed has at least three proper divisors.  No other integer can generate an infinite admissible sequence. \u220e"
