"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe possible values of \\(a_{1}\\) are exactly the integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6m\\qquad\\text{with }m\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all positive multiples of \\(6\\) whose co\u2011factor is coprime to \\(10\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a positive integer \\(N\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}<\\dots<d_{k}\n   \\]\n\n   be the proper divisors of \\(N\\) (so \\(d_{1}=1\\)).  \n   The three largest proper divisors are \\(d_{k-2},d_{k-1},d_{k}\\).  \n   They are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{N}{p},\\;\\frac{N}{r},\\;\\frac{N}{s},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(p<r<s\\) are the three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) that exceed \\(1\\).\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(N):=a_{n+1}=N\\Bigl(\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Fixed points.**  \n   The equality \\(f(N)=N\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The only triple of positive integers satisfying (2) with \\(p<r<s\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   (p,r,s)=(2,3,6).\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently a number \\(N\\) is a fixed point of the map \\(f\\) iff its three\n   smallest divisors larger than \\(1\\) are precisely \\(2,3,6\\).  \n   This happens exactly when  \n\n   * \\(2\\mid N\\) but \\(4\\nmid N\\) (so the exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(N\\) is \\(1\\));\n   * \\(3\\mid N\\);\n   * \\(5\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(5\\) would be a divisor between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\)).\n\n   Hence the fixed points are  \n\n   \\[\n   N=6m\\quad\\text{with }m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For every such \\(N\\) we have \\(f(N)=N\\); the sequence stays constant,\n   and each term has at least three proper divisors (namely \\(1,2,3\\)).\n\n3. **Behaviour of other numbers.**  \n   Let \\(N\\) be any positive integer possessing at least three proper\n   divisors and let \\(p<r<s\\) be defined as above.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is odd.*  \n   Then \\(p\\ge 3\\), whence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac13+\\frac13+\\frac13=1,\n   \\]\n\n   with strict inequality because the three divisors cannot all equal \\(3\\).\n   Thus \\(f(N)<N\\). Repeated application yields a strictly decreasing\n   sequence of positive integers, which must eventually reach a number with\n   at most two proper divisors (a prime or a square of a prime). Hence an\n   odd starting value cannot generate an infinite admissible sequence.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is even and \\(4\\mid N\\).*  \n   Then \\(p=2,\\;r=4\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n   \\]\n\n   so again \\(f(N)<N\\). The same decreasing\u2011argument as for odd numbers\n   shows that such an \\(N\\) cannot survive indefinitely.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is even, \\(2\\mid N\\) but \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(3\\nmid N\\).*  \n   Here \\(p=2,\\;r\\ge5\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac16<1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the sequence strictly decreases, leading to failure as before.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is even, \\(2\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N\\) but either \\(4\\mid N\\) or \\(5\\mid N\\).*  \n   The three smallest divisors are \\((2,3,4)\\) or \\((2,3,5)\\).  \n   In both cases  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\dfrac{13}{12}>1 &\\text{for }(2,3,4),\\\\[4pt]\n   \\dfrac{31}{30}>1 &\\text{for }(2,3,5).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(f(N)>\\!N\\). Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   f(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N\\quad\\text{or}\\quad f(N)=\\frac{31}{30}N,\n   \\]\n\n   which is an odd integer (the factor \\(\\frac{13}{12}\\) or \\(\\frac{31}{30}\\)\n   removes the factor \\(2\\)). The resulting odd term then falls under the\n   first case and inevitably leads to a number with fewer than three proper\n   divisors. Consequently such an \\(N\\) also cannot generate an infinite\n   admissible sequence.\n\n   The exhaustive case analysis shows that **every** integer possessing at\n   least three proper divisors but **not** of the form (3) eventually produces\n   a term with fewer than three proper divisors, and therefore cannot be a\n   valid \\(a_{1}\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible initial values are precisely the fixed points\n   described in (3). For each of them the sequence is constant, satisfying\n   the required condition for all \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{1},a_{2},\\dots\n\\]\n\nbe the infinite sequence defined in the statement.\nFor a positive integer \\(N\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n1=d_{0}<d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots<d_{k}=N\n\\]\n\nthe complete list of its divisors.  \nThe proper divisors are \\(d_{0},d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\); the three largest\nproper divisors are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{p},\\qquad\\frac{N}{r},\\qquad\\frac{N}{s},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\np=d_{1},\\; r=d_{2},\\; s=d_{3}\n\\]\n\nare the three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) that exceed \\(1\\).\nHence, for every \\(n\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\na_{n+1}=a_{n}\\Bigl(\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\Bigr),\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p<r<s\\) are the three smallest divisors of \\(a_{n}\\) larger than\n\\(1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Fixed points of the transformation\n\nEquation (1) yields a fixed point precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe only triple of positive integers satisfying (2) with\n\\(p<r<s\\) is \\((p,r,s)=(2,3,6)\\):\nindeed \\(1/2+1/3+1/6=1\\), and any other choice makes the left\u2013hand side\ndifferent from \\(1\\).\n\nThus a number \\(N\\) is a fixed point of the map \\(N\\mapsto a_{n+1}\\) iff\nits three smallest divisors larger than \\(1\\) are exactly \\(2,3,6\\).\nThis occurs exactly when\n\n* \\(2\\mid N\\) but \\(4\\nmid N\\) (the exponent of \\(2\\) in the prime\n  factorisation of \\(N\\) equals \\(1\\));\n* \\(3\\mid N\\);\n* \\(5\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(5\\) would be a divisor between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\)).\n\nConsequently the set of fixed points is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{S=\\{\\,6m\\mid m\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m\\,\\}}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(N\\in S\\) we have \\(a_{n+1}=N\\) for all \\(n\\); each term has at\nleast the proper divisors \\(1,2,3\\). Hence every element of \\(S\\) is an\nadmissible initial value.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Behaviour of all other numbers\n\nAssume that a positive integer \\(N\\) has at least three proper divisors but\n\\(N\\notin S\\).  Let \\(p<r<s\\) be defined as above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Odd numbers\n\nIf \\(N\\) is odd then \\(p\\ge 3\\).  Because the three numbers \\(p,r,s\\) are\ndistinct, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s<\\frac13+\\frac13+\\frac13=1,\n\\]\n\nso by (1) \\(f(N)=a_{n+1}<N\\).  Repeated application yields a strictly\ndecreasing sequence of positive integers; it must eventually reach a number\nwith at most two proper divisors (a prime or a square of a prime).  Such a\nterm violates the hypothesis that every term possesses at least three\nproper divisors.  Hence no odd integer can serve as \\(a_{1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Even numbers with a divisor \\(4\\)\n\nSuppose \\(4\\mid N\\).  Then \\(p=2,\\;r=4\\) and \\(s\\ge5\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n\\]\n\nso again \\(f(N)<N\\) and the same decreasing argument as in the odd case\nexcludes such \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Even numbers not divisible by \\(3\\)\n\nAssume \\(2\\mid N,\\;4\\nmid N\\) and \\(3\\nmid N\\).  \nThen \\(p=2\\) and \\(r\\ge5\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac16<1,\n\\]\n\nand the sequence strictly decreases, leading to a contradiction as before.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Even numbers divisible by \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) but not belonging to\n\\(S\\)\n\nNow let \\(2\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N\\) and \\(N\\notin S\\).  Because \\(N\\notin S\\),\neither \\(4\\mid N\\) or \\(5\\mid N\\).\n\n*If \\(4\\mid N\\):* the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,4\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14=\\frac{13}{12}>1,\n\\]\n\nand from (1)\n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(N\\) contains the factor \\(12\\), the quotient \\(\\frac{13}{12}N\\) is\nan odd integer.  The odd term falls under case\u202f2.1 and eventually yields a\nterm with fewer than three proper divisors.\n\n*If \\(5\\mid N\\) (and \\(4\\nmid N\\)):* the three smallest divisors are\n\\(2,3,5\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15=\\frac{31}{30}>1,\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{31}{30}N,\n\\]\n\nwhich is again odd (because \\(30\\mid N\\)).  The subsequent odd term leads,\nas before, to a failure.\n\nThus every even integer that is not of the form (3) eventually produces a\nterm with fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the requirement\nthat the sequence be infinite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe analysis shows:\n\n* Every integer of the form \\(6m\\) with \\(m\\) odd and \\(5\\nmid m\\) is a\n  fixed point of the recurrence; the constant sequence satisfies the\n  condition that each term has at least three proper divisors.\n\n* Any other integer possessing at least three proper divisors inevitably,\n  after finitely many steps, yields a term with at most two proper divisors,\n  which is forbidden.\n\nHence the admissible initial values are precisely the numbers described in\n(3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle a_{1}=6m\\quad\\text{where }m\\text{ is a positive odd integer not divisible by }5}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(a_{1}\\) is any positive multiple of \\(6\\) whose co\u2011factor is\ncoprime to \\(10\\).  For each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant,\n\\(a_{n}=a_{1}\\) for all \\(n\\), and every term has at least three proper\ndivisors. No other starting value can generate an infinite sequence with\nthe required property."
