\n\nand the sequence strictly decreases, leading to a contradiction as before.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Even numbers divisible by \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) but not belonging to\n\\(S\\)\n\nNow let \\(2\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N\\) and \\(N\\notin S\\).  Because \\(N\\notin S\\),\neither \\(4\\mid N\\) or \\(5\\mid N\\).\n\n*If \\(4\\mid N\\):* the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,4\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14=\\frac{13}{12}>1,\n\\]\n\nand from (1)\n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(N\\) contains the factor \\(12\\), the quotient \\(\\frac{13}{12}N\\) is\nan odd integer.  The odd term falls under case 2.1 and eventually yields a\nterm with fewer than three proper divisors.\n\n*If \\(5\\mid N\\) (and \\(4\\nmid N\\)):* the three smallest divisors are\n\\(2,3,5\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac15=\\frac{31}{30}>1,\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{31}{30}N,\n\\]\n\nwhich is again odd (because \\(30\\mid N\\)).  The subsequent odd term leads,\nas before, to a failure.\n\nThus every even integer that is not of the form (3) eventually produces a\nterm with fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the requirement\nthat the sequence be infinite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe analysis shows:\n\n* Every integer of the form \\(6m\\) with \\(m\\) odd and \\(5\\nmid m\\) is a\n  fixed point of the recurrence; the constant sequence satisfies the\n  condition that each term has at least three proper divisors.\n\n* Any other integer possessing at least three proper divisors inevitably,\n  after finitely many steps, yields a term with at most two proper divisors,\n  which is forbidden.\n\nHence the admissible initial values are precisely the numbers described in\n(3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\