"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible initial values are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6m\\qquad\\text{where }m\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;m\\text{ is odd and }5\\nmid m } .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(a_{1}\\) is any positive multiple of \\(6\\) whose co\u2011factor is coprime to \\(10\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a positive integer \\(N\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   1=d_{0}<d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots <d_{k}=N\n   \\]\n\n   be all its divisors.  \n   The three *largest* proper divisors are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{N}{p},\\;\\frac{N}{r},\\;\\frac{N}{s},\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   p=d_{1},\\;r=d_{2},\\;s=d_{3}\n   \\]\n\n   are the three *smallest* divisors of \\(N\\) that exceed \\(1\\).  \n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1}=a_{n}\\Bigl(\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Fixed points.**  \n   Equation (1) gives a fixed point iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The only triple of positive integers with \\(p<r<s\\) satisfying (2) is\n   \\((p,r,s)=(2,3,6)\\).  \n   Therefore a number \\(N\\) is a fixed point of the map \\(N\\mapsto a_{n+1}\\)\n   precisely when its three smallest divisors larger than \\(1\\) are\n   \\(2,3,6\\).  \n   This happens exactly when  \n\n   * the exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(N\\) is \\(1\\) (so \\(4\\nmid N\\));\n   * \\(3\\mid N\\);\n   * \\(5\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(5\\) would lie between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\)).\n\n   Consequently the set of fixed points is  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{\\,6m\\mid m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m\\,\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(N\\in S\\) we have \\(a_{n+1}=N\\) for all \\(n\\); each term has\n   at least the proper divisors \\(1,2,3\\). Hence every element of \\(S\\) is\n   an admissible initial value.\n\n3. **Numbers with exactly three proper divisors.**  \n   If a positive integer has exactly three proper divisors, then it is either\n   a product of two distinct primes, \\(N=pq\\;(p<q)\\), or a cube of a prime,\n   \\(N=p^{3}\\).\n\n   *For \\(N=pq\\):* the three proper divisors are \\(1,p,q\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1}=1+p+q.\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   1+p+q<N\\quad\\text{unless }(p,q)=(2,3),\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(N-p-q-1=(p-1)(q-1)-2\\ge0\\) with equality only for \\((2,3)\\).\n   Thus the sequence strictly decreases, and a decreasing sequence of\n   positive integers cannot be infinite; it must eventually reach a number\n   with at most two proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.\n   The exceptional case \\((p,q)=(2,3)\\) gives \\(N=6\\), which belongs to\n   the set \\(S\\).\n\n   *For \\(N=p^{3}\\):* the proper divisors are \\(1,p,p^{2}\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1}=1+p+p^{2}<p^{3}=N\\quad(p\\ge2).\n   \\]\n\n   Again the sequence decreases and cannot stay infinite.\n\n   Hence the only number with exactly three proper divisors that can start an\n   infinite admissible sequence is \\(6\\), already contained in \\(S\\).\n\n4. **All other numbers (at least four proper divisors).**  \n   Let \\(N\\) have at least four proper divisors and let \\(p<r<s\\) be defined\n   as in (1).  We consider all possibilities for the smallest divisors.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is odd:* then \\(p\\ge3\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s<\\frac13+\\frac13+\\frac13=1,\n   \\]\n\n   so by (1) \\(a_{n+1}<N\\).  The sequence is strictly decreasing; after\n   finitely many steps it reaches a number with at most two proper divisors,\n   which is impossible.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is even and \\(4\\mid N\\):* then \\(p=2,\\;r=4\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n   \\]\n\n   and again the sequence strictly decreases, leading to a contradiction.\n\n   *If \\(N\\) is even, \\(2\\mid N\\) but \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(3\\nmid N\\):* then\n   \\(p=2,\\;r\\ge5\\).  Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac16<1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the same decreasing argument applies.\n\n   *If \\(2\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N\\) but either \\(4\\mid N\\) or \\(5\\mid N\\):*  \n   the three smallest divisors are either \\((2,3,4)\\) or \\((2,3,5)\\).  In\n   both cases  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s>1,\n   \\]\n\n   giving  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{n+1}= \\frac{13}{12}N\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a_{n+1}= \\frac{31}{30}N .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(12\\mid N\\) (resp. \\(30\\mid N\\)), the new term is an odd\n   integer.  An odd term falls under the first case, so the sequence then\n   strictly decreases and eventually reaches a number with at most two proper\n   divisors.  Hence such an \\(N\\) cannot generate an infinite admissible\n   sequence.\n\n   The exhaustive case analysis shows that **every** integer possessing at\n   least three proper divisors but not belonging to the set \\(S\\) inevitably\n   produces a term with at most two proper divisors, contradicting the\n   requirement that the sequence be infinite.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only possible initial values are the fixed points described in (3);\n   for each of them the sequence is constant and satisfies the condition that\n   every term has at least three proper divisors. No other starting value can\n   do so.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{1},a_{2},\\dots\n\\]\n\nbe the infinite sequence defined in the problem.\nFor a positive integer \\(N\\) write its divisors in increasing order\n\n\\[\n1=d_{0}<d_{1}<d_{2}<\\dots<d_{k}=N .\n\\]\n\nThe proper divisors are \\(d_{0},d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k-1}\\); the three largest\nproper divisors are therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{p},\\qquad\\frac{N}{r},\\qquad\\frac{N}{s},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\np=d_{1},\\;r=d_{2},\\;s=d_{3}\n\\]\n\nare the three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) that exceed \\(1\\).\nHence for every \\(n\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\na_{n+1}=a_{n}\\Bigl(\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Fixed points\n\nEquation (1) yields a fixed point precisely when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s=1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe only triple of positive integers with \\(p<r<s\\) satisfying (2) is\n\\((p,r,s)=(2,3,6)\\) because  \n\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac16=1,\n\\]\nand any other choice makes the sum different from \\(1\\).\n\nThus a number \\(N\\) is a fixed point of the transformation \\(N\\mapsto a_{n+1}\\)\niff the three smallest divisors of \\(N\\) larger than \\(1\\) are exactly\n\\(2,3,6\\).  This occurs exactly when\n\n* the exponent of \\(2\\) in the prime factorisation of \\(N\\) equals \\(1\\)\n  (so \\(4\\nmid N\\));\n* \\(3\\mid N\\);\n* \\(5\\nmid N\\) (otherwise \\(5\\) would lie between \\(3\\) and \\(6\\)).\n\nConsequently the set of fixed points is  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\,6m\\mid m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m\\,\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(N\\in S\\) we have \\(a_{n+1}=N\\) for all \\(n\\); each term possesses\nthe proper divisors \\(1,2,3\\), so the required condition is satisfied.\nHence every element of \\(S\\) is an admissible initial value.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Numbers with exactly three proper divisors\n\nA positive integer has exactly three proper divisors iff it is either\n\n* a product of two distinct primes, \\(N=pq\\;(p<q)\\), or\n* a cube of a prime, \\(N=p^{3}\\).\n\n*If \\(N=pq\\):* the proper divisors are \\(1,p,q\\) and  \n\n\\[\na_{n+1}=1+p+q.\n\\]\n\nWe have  \n\n\\[\n1+p+q<N\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad(p-1)(q-1)>2,\n\\]\n\nwhich fails only for \\((p,q)=(2,3)\\).  Thus for all such \\(N\\) except\n\\(N=6\\) the sequence strictly decreases, and a decreasing sequence of\npositive integers cannot be infinite; after finitely many steps it reaches a\nnumber with at most two proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.\nThe exceptional case \\(N=6\\) belongs to \\(S\\).\n\n*If \\(N=p^{3}\\):* the proper divisors are \\(1,p,p^{2}\\) and  \n\n\\[\na_{n+1}=1+p+p^{2}<p^{3}=N\\qquad(p\\ge2).\n\\]\n\nAgain the sequence decreases and cannot stay infinite.\n\nHence the only integer with exactly three proper divisors that can start an\ninfinite admissible sequence is \\(6\\), already contained in \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  All other numbers (at least four proper divisors)\n\nAssume now that \\(N\\) has at least four proper divisors and let\n\\(p<r<s\\) be defined as in (1).  We examine all possibilities for the\nsmallest divisors.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(N\\) odd.*  \nThen \\(p\\ge3\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s<\\frac13+\\frac13+\\frac13=1,\n\\]\n\nso by (1) \\(a_{n+1}<N\\).  The sequence is strictly decreasing; after finitely\nmany steps it reaches a number with at most two proper divisors, which is\nimpossible.\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(N\\) even and \\(4\\mid N\\).*  \nHere \\(p=2,\\;r=4\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n\\]\n\nso again \\(a_{n+1}<N\\) and the same decreasing argument applies.\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 \\(N\\) even, \\(2\\mid N\\) but \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(3\\nmid N\\).*  \nThen \\(p=2,\\;r\\ge5\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac16<1,\n\\]\n\nand the sequence strictly decreases, leading to a contradiction as before.\n\n*Case 4 \u2013 \\(2\\mid N,\\;3\\mid N\\) and either \\(4\\mid N\\) or \\(5\\mid N\\).*  \nThe three smallest divisors are either \\((2,3,4)\\) or \\((2,3,5)\\).  In both\nsituations  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1p+\\frac1r+\\frac1s>1,\n\\]\n\ngiving respectively  \n\n\\[\na_{n+1}= \\frac{13}{12}N\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a_{n+1}= \\frac{31}{30}N .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(12\\mid N\\) (resp. \\(30\\mid N\\)), the new term is an odd integer.\nAn odd term falls under Case\u202f1, so the sequence then strictly decreases and\neventually reaches a number with at most two proper divisors.  Hence numbers\nin this case cannot generate an infinite admissible sequence.\n\nThe four cases exhaust all possibilities for a number possessing at least\nfour proper divisors but not belonging to the set \\(S\\).  In each case the\nsequence inevitably produces a term with at most two proper divisors, which\ncontradicts the requirement that the sequence be infinite.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe only initial values that allow an infinite sequence in which every term\nhas at least three proper divisors are the fixed points described in (3).\nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence is constant:\n\\(a_{n}=a_{1}\\) for all \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle a_{1}=6m\\ \\text{with }m\\text{ odd and }5\\nmid m } .\n\\]\n\nThese and only these values satisfy the conditions of the problem."
